<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thaba Bosiu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Thaba Bosigo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thaba Bosiu is a flat-topped sandstone mountain near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maseru">Maseru</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesotho">Lesotho</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe I</a> made his stronghold and capital in 1824. It withstood attacks by rivals and by Orange Free State commandos in the wars of 1865–1868. Moshoeshoe is buried on its summit, and it is a national monument regarded as the birthplace of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a> nation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thaba-bosiu.jpg" alt="Thaba Bosiu" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marduk · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:685_Thaba_Bosiu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Thaba Bosiu is a plateau of about 2 square kilometres, 1,804 metres above sea level, between the Orange and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caledon-river">Caledon Rivers</a> in the Maseru District, 24 km east of Maseru, in the valley of the Phuthiatsana River. It rises about 106 metres above the valley, and its summit is ringed by sheer cliffs some 12 metres high on average. It has eight springs and six passes, the main one being the Khubelu pass, and there is San rock art nearby. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a> believe the mountain has magical properties: earth taken from it, it is said, returns there overnight.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe I</a> and his people came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/butha-buthe">Butha-Buthe</a> in July 1824, escaping the Difaqane or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mfecane">Mfecane</a> wars. The name is loosely translated as mountain at night, since they arrived after dark, and Moshoeshoe spread word that the mountain grew larger at night to frighten his enemies. The plateau could hold livestock and stores for a long siege, and he gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and protection to refugees, many of whom stayed under his rule. In 1837 Private David Webber of the 72nd Seaforth Highlanders, given refuge there, built him a stone house of two rooms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mzilikazi">Mzilikazi</a> attacked the mountain without success.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> of the Orange Free State began a siege on 18 August 1865. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louw-wepener">Louw Wepener</a> led the assault behind an artillery bombardment, but only about 100 of his men were still with him by 5 pm, and he was shot at the top of the Khubelu pass, since also called Wepener's pass: the only enemy ever to reach the summit. The siege lasted until January 1866. Starving, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a> signed a treaty in April 1866 giving up three thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and more than two-thirds of their arable land. After the Third Free State–Basotho War, Moshoeshoe sought British protection, granted in March 1868 on the eve of a new Boer attack.</p><p>On 27 December 1966 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-ii">Moshoeshoe II</a>, who contested the legitimacy of Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leabua-jonathan">Leabua Jonathan</a>'s government and his own lack of executive power, held protest meetings that ended in a prayer meeting on the mountain. Jonathan banned it as a promotion of insurrection. Clashes with the security forces left 10 dead, opposition leaders were arrested, and the king, under house arrest, had to promise not to address public gatherings without the government's consent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesotho">Lesotho</a> declared the mountain a national monument in 1967, and in the 1990s the government began, with international development support, a programme to preserve and present it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-ii">Moshoeshoe II</a> was buried on the summit beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe I</a> in 1996. A cultural village, a conference centre and lodgings stand at its foot, below the historic site on the plateau.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thaba Bosiu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the war of 1865–1866 the commandos failed to storm the mountain fortress of <b>Thaba Bosiu</b>, where the commandant Louw Wepener was killed in 1865, but they devastated Basotho fields and herds and forced Moshoeshoe to cede land in the Treaty of Thaba Bosiu in 1866.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Moshoeshoe, a junior Kwena chief, gathered followers at Butha-Buthe in the early 1820s and in 1824 moved them to the flat-topped mountain of <b>Thaba Bosiu</b>, which proved impregnable.</blockquote><a href="/state/basotho-kingdom">Basotho Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
